      "content": "I apologize for the cut-off earlier. Let's continue with the scenario: imagine a beneficiary who has a \"domicile\" in Spain, but also has a significant connection to the UK through family ties or business interests. In this case, the UK IHT rules may still apply, but the beneficiary may be able to claim a \"foreign domicile exemption\" if they can demonstrate that their primary residence and ties are in Spain.\n\nNow, regarding the offshore accounts, you're right that they can be tricky to track down. The UK government's authority to tax offshore assets is based on the concept of \"worldwide assets.\" This means that the UK can tax assets that are held in any jurisdiction, not just those within the UK.\n\nThe UK's tax authorities, HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC), have a range of powers to gather information about offshore assets, including:\n\n1. Automatic Exchange of Information (AEOI): Many countries, including Spain, have signed agreements with the UK to exchange information about bank accounts and other financial assets.\n2. Common Reporting Standard (CRS): This is a global standard for the automatic exchange of financial account information, which includes offshore accounts.\n3. HMRC's own investigations: HMRC has the power",
